The PQXDH model in this repository is written in
[VCV-io](https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/VCV-io/tree/88efe9715a9fa9e7d89635c38cf70259836766c2),
a Lean framework for cryptographic proofs. This chapter introduces the parts of
VCV-io that the later chapters use. VCV-io keeps three notions apart.

1. A computation is a tree of oracle queries. It records which queries it makes and
   how the computation continues after each answer. It does not say how a query is answered.
2. A query implementation answers the queries inside a monad. Running a computation
   with an implementation gives a computation in that monad.
3. A probability semantics turns a computation into a measure on its outputs. Failure
   is the mass that the measure does not have.

The first four sections follow this order. The fifth section defines runtimes and the
advantages of an adversary against a UAKE scheme. Its last four cards use the UAKE
security experiment, which the next chapter defines.

The cards in the first four sections, and the first three cards of the fifth section, show
upstream code at the pinned commits of VCV-io and
[cslib](https://github.com/leanprover/cslib/tree/5b56df99362532c46e1175381fbbdd755bd56030).
The exceptions are the cards labelled "Book example" in the second and fourth sections. They
show `ProbTrees.lean`, a small file of this book that works out three programs as trees. The
last four cards of the fifth section come from this repository.

# Oracle specifications

A cryptographic game gives its adversary access to oracles. VCV-io describes the oracles
by the types of their queries and of their answers.

::::definition "oracle_spec" (lean := "OracleSpec")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair OracleSpec -docstrings
Let $`I` be a type. An _oracle specification_ with index type $`I` is a family of types
$`R = (R(i))_{i \in I}`. We call an element $`i \in I` a _query_ and $`R(i)` the type of
_answers_ to $`i`.
:::
::::

In Lean, `OracleSpec ι` is the function type `ι → Type v`. For a specification `spec`,
VCV-io writes `spec.Domain` for the index type and `spec.Range i` for the answer type of
the query `i`.

::::definition "oracle_spec_to_pfunctor" (lean := "OracleSpec.toPFunctor") (uses := "oracle_spec")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair OracleSpec.toPFunctor -docstrings
A _polynomial functor_ $`P` consists of a type $`A` of _positions_ and, for each
$`a \in A`, a type $`B(a)` of _directions_. It acts on types by
$$`P(X) = \sum_{a \in A} X^{B(a)}.`
Let $`R` be an oracle specification with index type $`I`. The _polynomial functor of_
$`R` is the polynomial functor $`P_R` with positions $`I` and directions $`R(i)` at
$`i \in I`.
:::
::::

An element of $`P_R(X)` is a query $`i` together with a map $`R(i) \to X`, which says how
to continue for each possible answer. In Lean, the inverse construction is
`OracleSpec.ofPFunctor`, and the lemmas `toPFunctor_ofPFunctor` and
`ofPFunctor_toPFunctor` show that the two constructions are inverse to each other.

# Computations as free monads

An oracle computation is a tree. Each inner node is a query, each answer to it leads to a
subtree, and each leaf is an output.

::::definition "free_monad" (lean := "PFunctor.FreeM")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair PFunctor.FreeM -docstrings
Let $`P` be a polynomial functor with positions $`A` and directions $`B`. For a type
$`\alpha`, we define $`\mathrm{Free}_P(\alpha)` to be the inductive type generated by two
constructors:

* $`\mathrm{pure}(x)` for $`x \in \alpha`;
* $`\mathrm{liftBind}(a, k)` for $`a \in A` and $`k : B(a) \to \mathrm{Free}_P(\alpha)`.

We call $`\mathrm{Free}_P` the _free monad_ on $`P`.
:::
::::

Equivalently, $`\mathrm{Free}_P(\alpha)` is the least solution of
$`T \cong \alpha + P(T)`. A path from the root to a leaf chooses one answer at every node
on the way, so it is a transcript of the interaction. In Lean, the type is cslib's
`PFunctor.FreeM`. VCV-io reaches it through
[PolyFun](https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/PolyFun/tree/ef4c157b3c6775fb55864303d96f9b400428d5f0),
which re-exports it.

::::definition "free_bind" (lean := "PFunctor.FreeM.bind") (uses := "free_monad")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair PFunctor.FreeM.bind -docstrings
Let $`P` be a polynomial functor, and let $`\alpha` and $`\beta` be types. For
$`t \in \mathrm{Free}_P(\alpha)` and $`f : \alpha \to \mathrm{Free}_P(\beta)`, we define
$`t \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} f \in \mathrm{Free}_P(\beta)` by recursion on $`t`:
$$`\mathrm{pure}(x) \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} f = f(x), \qquad
\mathrm{liftBind}(a, k) \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} f = \mathrm{liftBind}(a,\, b \mapsto k(b) \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} f).`
:::
::::

So $`t \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} f` replaces each leaf $`\mathrm{pure}(x)` of $`t` with the tree
$`f(x)`. A monad is _lawful_ if $`\mathrm{pure}` is a left and right unit for
$`\mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=}` and $`\mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=}` is associative. With $`\mathrm{pure}`
as unit, this bind makes $`\mathrm{Free}_P` a lawful monad. In Lean, these are the
instances `Monad` and `LawfulMonad` of `PFunctor.FreeM`.

::::definition "oracle_comp" (lean := "OracleComp") (uses := "oracle_spec_to_pfunctor, free_monad")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair OracleComp -docstrings
Let $`R` be an oracle specification and let $`\alpha` be a type. An _oracle computation_
for $`R` with outputs in $`\alpha` is an element of $`\mathrm{Free}_{P_R}(\alpha)`.
:::
::::

In Lean, `OracleComp spec` is by definition `PFunctor.FreeM spec.toPFunctor`. A single
query with its continuation, an element of $`P_R(X)`, has its own type `OracleQuery spec`.

::::definition "prob_comp" (lean := "ProbComp") (uses := "oracle_comp")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbComp -docstrings
Let $`\mathrm{unif}` be the oracle specification with index type $`\mathbb{N}` and
$`\mathrm{unif}(n) = \{0, 1, \dots, n\}`, and let $`\alpha` be a type. A _probabilistic
computation_ with outputs in $`\alpha` is an oracle computation for $`\mathrm{unif}` with
outputs in $`\alpha`. We write
$`\mathrm{ProbComp}(\alpha) := \mathrm{Free}_{P_{\mathrm{unif}}}(\alpha)` for the type of
these computations.
:::
::::

The query $`n` asks for an element of $`\{0, \dots, n\}`. The definition does not say how
the answer is chosen. The fourth section gives the semantics in which it is uniform. In
Lean, $`\mathrm{unif}` is `unifSpec`, with `Fin (n + 1)` for $`\{0, \dots, n\}`, and
`ProbComp` is an abbreviation for `OracleComp unifSpec`. For a finite nonempty type `T`,
an instance of the class `SampleableType` gives a probabilistic computation `$ᵗ T` whose
output is uniform on `T` in that semantics.

## Programs as trees

For `ProbComp`, the tree of a computation is easy to draw. A node carries a query
$`n \in \mathbb{N}`, which asks for an element of $`\{0, \dots, n\}`, and it has one child for
each of the $`n + 1` possible answers. A leaf carries an output. A path from the root to a leaf
lists the answers along the way. Every path is finite because $`\mathrm{Free}_P(\alpha)` is an
inductive type, and every node has finitely many children, so a computation has finitely many
nodes. This follows by induction on the tree: a leaf is one node, and a node with $`n + 1`
children has one node more than its children together.

In a `do` block, a draw such as `x ← uniformFin n` is a node, and `return v` is a leaf. A step
that asks no query adds no node. By the first equation of bind,
$`\mathrm{pure}(x) \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} f = f(x)`, so such a step makes no node of its own. The
child of a node at an answer is the computation that the rest of the program gives for that
answer, so an `if` on an earlier answer chooses the subtree. The three programs of this section
draw only from $`\{0, 1\}`, and we write $`\mathrm{node}(k)` for a node of this kind.

::::definition "draw_node" (lean := "ProbTrees.node") (uses := "free_monad, prob_comp")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbTrees.node -docstrings
Let $`\alpha` be a type and let $`k : \{0, 1\} \to \mathrm{ProbComp}(\alpha)`. We define
$$`\mathrm{node}(k) := \mathrm{liftBind}(1, k) \in \mathrm{ProbComp}(\alpha).`
The root of $`\mathrm{node}(k)` is the query $`1`, and its subtree at the answer
$`x \in \{0, 1\}` is $`k(x)`.
:::
::::

In the pictures, a node is a rounded box labelled "draw". The semantics of the fourth section
makes its answer uniform on $`\{0, 1\}`.

::::lemma_ "draw_bind" (lean := "ProbTrees.draw_eq, ProbTrees.node_eq_bind") (uses := "draw_node, free_bind")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbTrees.draw_eq (part := "signature") -docstrings
Let $`\mathrm{draw} \in \mathrm{ProbComp}(\{0, 1\})` be the computation that makes the query
$`1` and returns the answer. Then $`\mathrm{draw} = \mathrm{node}(\mathrm{pure})`.
:::
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbTrees.node_eq_bind (part := "signature") -docstrings
For every type $`\alpha` and every $`k : \{0, 1\} \to \mathrm{ProbComp}(\alpha)`,
$$`\mathrm{node}(k) = \mathrm{draw} \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} k.`
:::
::::

In Lean, $`\mathrm{draw}` is `uniformFin 1`, and both equations hold by `rfl`. They also follow
from the equations of bind: $`\mathrm{node}(\mathrm{pure}) \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} k` is
$`\mathrm{liftBind}(1, x \mapsto \mathrm{pure}(x) \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} k)`, and
$`\mathrm{pure}(x) \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} k = k(x)`.

The first program draws once and returns the answer twice.

::::definition "program_t1" (lean := "ProbTrees.t₁") (uses := "draw_bind")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.treePair ProbTrees.t₁ (figure := "t1") -docstrings -comments
Let $`t_1 \in \mathrm{ProbComp}(\mathrm{Bool} \times \mathrm{Bool})` be the computation that
draws $`x \in \{0, 1\}` and returns $`(x = 1,\, x = 1)`. Here $`x = 1` stands for a truth
value: it is $`\mathrm{true}` if $`x = 1` and $`\mathrm{false}` if $`x = 0`. Formally,
$$`t_1 = \mathrm{draw} \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} (x \mapsto \mathrm{pure}(x = 1,\, x = 1)).`
:::
::::

The second program draws twice. It returns the truth values of the two answers.

::::definition "program_t2" (lean := "ProbTrees.t₂") (uses := "draw_bind")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.treePair ProbTrees.t₂ (figure := "t2") -docstrings -comments
Let $`t_2 \in \mathrm{ProbComp}(\mathrm{Bool} \times \mathrm{Bool})` be the computation that
draws $`x \in \{0, 1\}`, then draws $`y \in \{0, 1\}`, and returns $`(x = 1,\, y = 1)`.
Formally,
$$`t_2 = \mathrm{draw} \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} \bigl(x \mapsto \mathrm{draw} \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} (y \mapsto \mathrm{pure}(x = 1,\, y = 1))\bigr).`
:::
::::

Both programs return a pair of truth values, and they differ in where the second value comes
from. In $`t_1` it is a copy of the first, so the tree has one node. In $`t_2` it comes from a
new draw, so each answer to the first draw leads to a second node.

The third program makes its second draw only after one of the two answers to the first. VCV-io
writes `$ᵗ Bool` for its sampler of $`\mathrm{Bool}`. The sampler is a draw from $`\{0, 1\}`,
and the answer $`0` stands for $`\mathrm{true}`.

::::lemma_ "uniform_bool" (lean := "ProbTrees.uniformBool_eq") (uses := "draw_node")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbTrees.uniformBool_eq (part := "signature") -docstrings
Let $`\mathrm{sample}_{\mathrm{Bool}} \in \mathrm{ProbComp}(\mathrm{Bool})` be the sampler of
$`\mathrm{Bool}` in VCV-io. Then
$$`\mathrm{sample}_{\mathrm{Bool}} = \mathrm{node}(i \mapsto \mathrm{pure}(i = 0)).`
That is, it draws $`i \in \{0, 1\}` and returns $`\mathrm{true}` if $`i = 0` and
$`\mathrm{false}` if $`i = 1`.
:::
::::

::::definition "program_t3" (lean := "ProbTrees.t₃") (uses := "uniform_bool")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.treePair ProbTrees.t₃ (figure := "t3") -docstrings -comments
Let $`t_3 \in \mathrm{ProbComp}(\mathrm{Bool})` be the computation that samples
$`x \in \mathrm{Bool}`. If $`x = \mathrm{true}`, it samples $`y \in \mathrm{Bool}`, and
otherwise it sets $`y = \mathrm{true}`. It returns $`y`.
:::
::::

In $`t_3`, the first answer decides whether there is a second node, so the paths have
different lengths. Programs whose later queries depend on earlier answers are called adaptive.
The next proposition gives the three trees as equations.

::::proposition "program_trees" (lean := "ProbTrees.t₁_eq, ProbTrees.t₂_eq, ProbTrees.t₃_eq") (uses := "program_t1, program_t2, program_t3, uniform_bool")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbTrees.t₁_eq (part := "signature") -docstrings
$$`t_1 = \mathrm{node}(x \mapsto \mathrm{pure}(x = 1,\, x = 1)).`
:::
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbTrees.t₂_eq (part := "signature") -docstrings
$$`\begin{aligned} t_2 = \mathrm{node}\bigl(x \mapsto{} & \mathrm{node}(y \mapsto \\ & \mathrm{pure}(x = 1,\, y = 1))\bigr). \end{aligned}`
:::
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbTrees.t₃_eq (part := "signature") -docstrings
$$`\begin{aligned} t_3 &= \mathrm{node}(i \mapsto k_3(i)),\\ k_3(0) &= \mathrm{node}(j \mapsto \mathrm{pure}(j = 0)),\\ k_3(1) &= \mathrm{pure}(\mathrm{true}). \end{aligned}`
:::
::::

The first two equations hold by the equations of bind and the lemma above, and in Lean they
are `rfl`. For $`t_3`, the proof rewrites both samplers with the lemma on
$`\mathrm{sample}_{\mathrm{Bool}}` and simplifies the `if`. The pictures draw these three
trees. An edge carries the answer, and a leaf carries the output. In the picture of $`t_3`, the
answers are labelled by truth values, with $`0` standing for $`\mathrm{true}`.

The leaves of a tree carry labels, and the nodes do not. We separate the two.

::::definition "tree_shape" (lean := "ProbTrees.shape") (uses := "free_bind")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbTrees.shape -docstrings
Let $`t \in \mathrm{ProbComp}(\alpha)`. The _shape_ of $`t` is the computation
$`\mathrm{shape}(t) \in \mathrm{ProbComp}(\mathrm{Unit})` that runs $`t` and returns $`()`, the
element of $`\mathrm{Unit}`, in place of the output of $`t`.
:::
::::

By the equations of bind, $`\mathrm{shape}(t)` is $`t` with every leaf $`\mathrm{pure}(x)`
replaced by $`\mathrm{pure}(())`. It has the nodes of $`t` and no information in its leaves.

::::lemma_ "shape_map" (lean := "ProbTrees.shape_map") (uses := "tree_shape")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbTrees.shape_map (part := "signature") -docstrings
Let $`f : \alpha \to \beta` and $`t \in \mathrm{ProbComp}(\alpha)`, and let
$`\mathrm{map}(f, t)` be the computation that runs $`t` and returns $`f` of its output. Then
$`\mathrm{shape}(\mathrm{map}(f, t)) = \mathrm{shape}(t)`.
:::
::::

So a map changes the labels of the leaves and keeps the shape. Conversely, a tree is
determined by its shape together with a labelling of the leaves of the shape. A leaf of
$`\mathrm{shape}(t)` is a path of answers from the root, and the label of this leaf is the
output of $`t` along the path. This holds by induction on $`t`. We do not formalise it, and
the pictures use it: each draws the shape and writes the label into each leaf.

::::proposition "program_shapes" (lean := "ProbTrees.shape_t₁, ProbTrees.shape_t₂, ProbTrees.shape_t₃") (uses := "tree_shape, program_trees")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbTrees.shape_t₁ (part := "signature") -docstrings
$$`\mathrm{shape}(t_1) = \mathrm{node}(x \mapsto \mathrm{pure}(())).`
:::
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbTrees.shape_t₂ (part := "signature") -docstrings
$$`\begin{aligned} \mathrm{shape}(t_2) = \mathrm{node}\bigl(x \mapsto{} & \mathrm{node}(y \mapsto \\ & \mathrm{pure}(()))\bigr). \end{aligned}`
:::
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbTrees.shape_t₃ (part := "signature") -docstrings
$$`\begin{aligned} \mathrm{shape}(t_3) &= \mathrm{node}(i \mapsto k_3'(i)),\\ k_3'(0) &= \mathrm{node}(j \mapsto \mathrm{pure}(())),\\ k_3'(1) &= \mathrm{pure}(()). \end{aligned}`
:::
::::

So $`t_1` has one node and two leaves, $`t_2` has three nodes and four leaves, and $`t_3` has
two nodes and three leaves. The fourth section computes the probability of each leaf.

# Implementing oracles

A query implementation gives each query a computation that produces its answer.

::::definition "query_impl" (lean := "QueryImpl") (uses := "oracle_spec")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair QueryImpl -docstrings
Let $`R` be an oracle specification with index type $`I`, and let $`m` be a map from
types to types. A _query implementation_ of $`R` in $`m` is a family
$`\theta = (\theta_i)_{i \in I}` with $`\theta_i \in m(R(i))`.
:::
::::

The definition needs no monad structure on $`m`. The simulation below does. In Lean,
`QueryImpl spec m` is the dependent function type `(x : spec.Domain) → m (spec.Range x)`.

::::definition "simulate_q" (lean := "simulateQ") (uses := "query_impl, oracle_comp")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair simulateQ -docstrings
Let $`R` be an oracle specification, let $`m` be a monad, and let $`\theta` be a query
implementation of $`R` in $`m`. For every type $`\alpha`, we define
$`\mathrm{sim}_\theta : \mathrm{Free}_{P_R}(\alpha) \to m(\alpha)` by recursion on trees:
$$`\mathrm{sim}_\theta(\mathrm{pure}(x)) = \mathrm{pure}(x), \qquad
\mathrm{sim}_\theta(\mathrm{liftBind}(i, k)) = \theta_i \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} (b \mapsto \mathrm{sim}_\theta(k(b))).`
:::
::::

So $`\mathrm{sim}_\theta` replaces each query node with the computation that $`\theta`
gives for it. In Lean, `simulateQ impl` is cslib's fold `PFunctor.FreeM.liftM impl`, whose
two defining equations are the two equations above.

::::theorem "simulate_q_bind" (lean := "simulateQ_bind") (uses := "simulate_q, free_bind")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair simulateQ_bind (part := "signature") -docstrings
Let $`R` be an oracle specification, let $`m` be a lawful monad, and let $`\theta` be a
query implementation of $`R` in $`m`. For all types $`\alpha` and $`\beta`, every
$`t \in \mathrm{Free}_{P_R}(\alpha)` and every $`f : \alpha \to \mathrm{Free}_{P_R}(\beta)`,
$$`\mathrm{sim}_\theta(t \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} f) = \mathrm{sim}_\theta(t) \mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=} (\mathrm{sim}_\theta \circ f).`
:::
::::

The equation $`\mathrm{sim}_\theta(\mathrm{pure}(x)) = \mathrm{pure}(x)` holds by definition
(`simulateQ_pure`). A _monad morphism_ from a monad $`m` to a monad $`m'` is a family of
maps $`m(\alpha) \to m'(\alpha)` that commutes with $`\mathrm{pure}` and with
$`\mathbin{>\!\!>\!\!=}`. With the theorem, the equation says that $`\mathrm{sim}_\theta`
is a monad morphism. PolyFun proves that it is the only monad morphism that agrees with
$`\theta` on single queries (`PFunctor.FreeM.liftMHom_unique`). This is the universal
property of the free monad: query implementations in a lawful monad $`m` are the same as monad morphisms
from $`\mathrm{Free}_{P_R}` to $`m`.

Security games use this to give an adversary its oracles. For example, the UAKE game of
the next chapter answers the adversary's queries in a state monad that holds the
sessions, and `simulateQ` turns the adversary into a computation in that monad.

# Output distributions and failure

A probability semantics for computations needs a distribution for the answers to each
query.

::::definition "free_denote" (lean := "PFunctor.FreeM.denote") (uses := "free_monad")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair PFunctor.FreeM.denote -docstrings
Let $`P` be a polynomial functor with positions $`A` and directions $`B`, where each
$`B(a)` is a measurable space. An _answer measure_ for $`P` is a family
$`\nu = (\nu_a)_{a \in A}` of probability measures, with $`\nu_a` a measure on $`B(a)`.

Let $`\nu` be an answer measure for $`P` and let $`\alpha` be a measurable space. For
$`t \in \mathrm{Free}_P(\alpha)` we define the _denotation_ $`[\![t]\!]`, a measure on
$`\alpha`, by recursion on $`t`:

* $`[\![\mathrm{pure}(x)]\!] = \delta_x`, the Dirac measure at $`x`;
* if the map $`b \mapsto [\![k(b)]\!]` is $`\nu_a`-almost everywhere measurable, then
  $`[\![\mathrm{liftBind}(a, k)]\!]` is the measure
  $`S \mapsto \int [\![k(b)]\!](S) \, d\nu_a(b)`, and otherwise it is the zero measure.
:::
::::

The measurability condition refers to the usual σ-algebra on the space of measures on
$`\alpha`. In Lean, an answer measure is an instance of the class `PFunctor.AnswerMeasure`,
the integral is the bind `Measure.bind` of the Giry monad, and the measurability test is an
`if` with `else 0`. For an oracle specification, VCV-io gives every answer type the
discrete σ-algebra, so every map out of an answer type is measurable and the test always
succeeds. For $`\mathrm{unif}`, the answer measure is the uniform measure on
$`\{0, \dots, n\}` (the instance `UniformAnswerMeasure.unifSpec`).

::::definition "eval_dist" (lean := "EvalDistSemantics")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair EvalDistSemantics -docstrings
Let $`m` be a map from types to types. A _measure semantics_ for $`m` assigns to every
measurable space $`\alpha` and every $`x \in m(\alpha)` a measure $`\mathcal{D}(x)` on
$`\alpha` with $`\mathcal{D}(x)(\alpha) \le 1`.
:::
::::

In Lean, a measure semantics is an instance of the class `EvalDistSemantics`, and
`evalDist mx`, written `𝒟[mx]`, is the measure $`\mathcal{D}(x)` that it gives `mx`. An
oracle specification with an answer measure gives its oracle computations the measure
semantics $`\mathcal{D}(t) = [\![t]\!]` (`PFunctor.FreeM.evalDist_eq_denote`). In
particular, every probabilistic computation $`x` has a measure $`\mathcal{D}(x)`, and the
later chapters use this semantics.

::::definition "pr_fail" (lean := "prFail") (uses := "eval_dist")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair prFail -docstrings
Let $`m` be a monad with a measure semantics $`\mathcal{D}`, let $`\alpha` be a type, and
let $`x \in m(\alpha)`. Let $`x'` be the computation in $`m` that runs $`x` and outputs
$`\top`, with outputs in the discrete space $`\{\top, \bot\}` of truth values. We define
the _failure probability_ of $`x` to be
$$`\Pr_{\mathrm{fail}}(x) := 1 - \mathcal{D}(x')(\{\top\}).`
:::
::::

The type $`\alpha` needs no σ-algebra. If $`m` is a lawful monad and $`\mathcal{D}`
commutes with measurable maps (`evalDist_map`), then
$`\Pr_{\mathrm{fail}}(x) = 1 - \mathcal{D}(x)(\alpha)` for every measurable space
$`\alpha`. So the failure probability is the mass that $`\mathcal{D}(x)` lacks. Oracle
computations satisfy both conditions. In Lean, the truth values are the type `Prop`, and
the lemma `prFail_def` restates the definition as `prFail mx = 1 - Pr{let _ ← mx}[True]`,
where `Pr{…}[…]` is VCV-io's notation for the probability of an event.

::::theorem "oracle_comp_lossless" (lean := "OracleComp.prFail_eq_zero") (uses := "pr_fail, oracle_comp")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair OracleComp.prFail_eq_zero (part := "signature") -docstrings
Let $`R` be an oracle specification with an answer measure $`\nu`, where every answer
type carries the discrete σ-algebra. Every oracle computation $`t` for $`R` has failure
probability $`0` in the measure semantics $`\mathcal{D}(t) = [\![t]\!]`, where
$`[\![t]\!]` is the denotation of $`t` for $`\nu`.
:::
::::

With the discrete σ-algebra, every node passes the measurability test of the denotation,
so no mass is lost. Failure therefore comes from the monad in which an experiment runs.
For example, the semantics of `OptionT m` pulls the measure of the underlying computation
back along `some`, so a result `none` is lost mass.

## Laws of the example programs

The semantics turns the trees of the second section into probabilities. At a node with the
query $`n`, the answer measure of $`\mathrm{unif}` is uniform on $`\{0, \dots, n\}`. By the
equation for $`\mathrm{liftBind}` in the denotation, each of the $`n + 1` subtrees has weight
$`1/(n+1)`. A leaf has the Dirac measure at its output. So in a tree whose nodes all ask the
query $`1`, a path through $`d` nodes has probability $`2^{-d}`, and an output $`v` has the sum
of the probabilities of the paths to the leaves labelled $`v`. This follows by induction on the
tree. The Lean proofs below do not use this rule. They compute each probability with two lemmas
of VCV-io: the formula `prEvent_bind_eq_sum_fintype` for the probability of an event after a
bind, and the formula `ProbComp.prEvent_uniformFin` for one draw.

In the proposition, $`\Pr[s = v]` is $`\mathcal{D}(s)(\{v\})`, as for the canonical runtime. In
Lean it is `Pr{let q ← s}[q = v]`, and `prEvent_eq_evalDist_of_discrete` identifies this
with the mass of the singleton.

::::theorem "program_laws" (lean := "ProbTrees.t₁_pr, ProbTrees.t₂_pr, ProbTrees.t₃_pr") (uses := "program_trees, prob_comp_runtime_canonical")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbTrees.t₁_pr (part := "signature") -docstrings
For all $`b, c \in \mathrm{Bool}`,
$$`\Pr[t_1 = (b, c)] = \begin{cases} 1/2 & \text{if } b = c,\\ 0 & \text{if } b \ne c. \end{cases}`
:::
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbTrees.t₂_pr (part := "signature") -docstrings
For all $`b, c \in \mathrm{Bool}`, $`\Pr[t_2 = (b, c)] = 1/4`.
:::
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbTrees.t₃_pr (part := "signature") -docstrings
For all $`b \in \mathrm{Bool}`,
$$`\Pr[t_3 = b] = \begin{cases} 3/4 & \text{if } b = \mathrm{true},\\ 1/4 & \text{if } b = \mathrm{false}. \end{cases}`
:::
::::

The pictures show why. In the tree of $`t_1`, each of the two leaves has probability $`1/2`,
and the pairs $`(\mathrm{false}, \mathrm{true})` and $`(\mathrm{true}, \mathrm{false})` do not
occur. In the tree of $`t_2`, each of the four leaves has probability $`1/4`. In the tree of
$`t_3`, the leaf under $`x = \mathrm{false}` is labelled $`\mathrm{true}` and has probability
$`1/2`. The two leaves under $`x = \mathrm{true}` have probability $`1/4` each, and they are
labelled $`\mathrm{true}` and $`\mathrm{false}`. So the output $`\mathrm{true}` has probability
$`1/2 + 1/4`.

The programs $`t_1` and $`t_2` return their two truth values in different ways, and the laws
show a difference between them. Each coordinate has the same law under both programs.

::::theorem "program_marginals" (lean := "ProbTrees.marginal_pr") (uses := "program_laws")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbTrees.marginal_pr (part := "signature") -docstrings
Let $`b \in \mathrm{Bool}`. For $`s = t_1` and for $`s = t_2`,
$$`\begin{aligned} \mathcal{D}(s)(\{q \mid q_1 = b\}) &= 1/2,\\ \mathcal{D}(s)(\{q \mid q_2 = b\}) &= 1/2. \end{aligned}`
:::
::::

In Lean, the four statements are conjuncts. Each is
`Pr{let q ← s}[q.1 = b] = 2⁻¹` or the same with `q.2`, by `prEvent_eq_evalDist_of_discrete`.
The joint laws differ.

::::theorem "program_joint" (lean := "ProbTrees.evalDist_ne") (uses := "program_laws")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbTrees.evalDist_ne (part := "signature") -docstrings
$$`\mathcal{D}(t_1) \ne \mathcal{D}(t_2).`
:::
::::

The proof compares the mass of $`(\mathrm{false}, \mathrm{true})`, which is $`0` under $`t_1`
and $`1/4` under $`t_2` by the laws above. The event that the two coordinates are equal also
separates the programs: adding the laws gives probability $`1` under $`t_1` and $`1/2` under
$`t_2`. So the laws of the coordinates do not determine the law of the pair. An experiment
that uses one coordinate cannot tell $`t_1` from $`t_2`, and an experiment that compares the
two coordinates can.

# Experiments and advantages

The experiments in this repository are written over an arbitrary monad $`m`. A runtime
gives the semantics of $`m` and a way to sample public randomness in $`m`.

::::definition "prob_comp_runtime" (lean := "ProbCompRuntime") (uses := "eval_dist, prob_comp")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbCompRuntime -docstrings
Let $`m` be a monad. A _runtime_ $`R` for $`m` consists of

1. a monad $`\mathrm{Sem}`, a monad morphism $`\mathrm{interp} : m \to \mathrm{Sem}`, and a
   map $`\mathrm{obs}` that assigns to every measurable space $`\alpha` and every
   $`y \in \mathrm{Sem}(\alpha)` a measure $`\mathrm{obs}(y)` on $`\alpha` with
   $`\mathrm{obs}(y)(\alpha) \le 1`;
2. a monad morphism $`\iota : \mathrm{ProbComp} \to m`,

such that the _semantics_ $`\mathcal{D}_R(x) := \mathrm{obs}(\mathrm{interp}(x))` of $`R`
satisfies $`\mathcal{D}_R(m(f)(x)) = f_* \mathcal{D}_R(x)` for all measurable spaces
$`\alpha` and $`\beta`, every measurable map $`f : \alpha \to \beta` and every
$`x \in m(\alpha)`. Here $`m(f)(x)` applies $`f` to the output of $`x`, and $`f_*` is the
push-forward of measures. For a discrete space $`\alpha`, a computation
$`x \in m(\alpha)` and $`v \in \alpha`, we write
$`\Pr_R[x = v] := \mathcal{D}_R(x)(\{v\})`.
:::
::::

In Lean, item 1 is the structure `SemanticsVia`, item 2 is the structure `ProbCompLift`,
and the condition is the field `evalDist_map_eq`. No field relates
$`\mathcal{D}_R \circ \iota` to the semantics $`\mathcal{D}` of probabilistic computations.
That condition is part of the coherence of a runtime, which the next chapter defines.

::::definition "prob_comp_runtime_canonical" (lean := "ProbCompRuntime.probComp") (uses := "prob_comp_runtime, eval_dist, free_denote")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair ProbCompRuntime.probComp -docstrings
Let $`\mathcal{D}` be the measure semantics of probabilistic computations given by the
answer measure that is uniform on $`\{0, \dots, n\}` for every query $`n`. The _canonical
runtime_ for probabilistic computations is the runtime for $`\mathrm{ProbComp}` with
$`\mathrm{Sem} = \mathrm{ProbComp}`, with $`\mathrm{interp}` and $`\iota` the identity, and
with $`\mathrm{obs} = \mathcal{D}`. For a probabilistic computation $`x` with outputs in a
discrete space $`\alpha` and $`v \in \alpha`, we write
$`\Pr[x = v] := \mathcal{D}(x)(\{v\})`.
:::
::::

The semantics of the canonical runtime is $`\mathcal{D}`, so $`\Pr[x = v]` is
$`\Pr_R[x = v]` for this runtime $`R`. In Lean, the lemma
`ProbCompRuntime.probComp_evalDist` states that the semantics of this runtime is `𝒟[·]`,
and it holds by definition. By the theorem on oracle computations above, no computation
has positive failure probability under this runtime. The PQXDH security theorems of this
repository are stated for this runtime.

::::definition "bool_bias" (lean := "MeasureTheory.Measure.boolBias")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair MeasureTheory.Measure.boolBias -docstrings
For $`a, b \in [0, \infty]`, put $`|a - b| := (a \mathbin{\dot-} b) + (b \mathbin{\dot-} a)`,
where $`\dot-` is truncated subtraction. Let $`\mu` be a measure on $`\{0, 1\}`. We define
the _bias_ of $`\mu` to be
$$`\mathrm{bias}(\mu) := |\mu\{1\} - \mu\{0\}| \in [0, \infty].`
:::
::::

When $`a` and $`b` are finite, $`|a - b|` is the usual absolute difference. In Lean,
$`\{0, 1\}` is the type `Bool`, with `true` for $`1`, and $`|a - b|` is
`ENNReal.absDiff`. For example, VCV-io's `KEMScheme.IND_CCA_Advantage` is
the bias of the IND-CCA game, which outputs whether the adversary guessed the challenge
bit.

The rest of this section measures adversaries against UAKE schemes. The next chapter
defines UAKE schemes, their adversaries and the security experiment
$`\mathsf{Exp}(\mathcal{A})`. The step numbers below refer to the steps of that experiment.

::::definition "uake_advantage" (lean := "AKE.UAKE.advantage") (uses := "prob_comp_runtime, uake_adversary, uake_exp")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair AKE.UAKE.advantage -docstrings
Let $`\Pi` be a UAKE scheme over a monad $`m` whose key space $`K` is finite and
nonempty, let $`R` be a runtime for $`m` with monad morphism $`\iota`, and let
$`\mathcal{A}` be an adversary against $`\Pi`. Let $`\mathsf{Exp}(\mathcal{A})` be the
security experiment of $`\Pi`, run with $`\iota`. The _advantage_ of $`\mathcal{A}` under
$`R` is the real number
$$`\mathrm{Adv}_R(\mathcal{A}) := \bigl|\Pr_R[\mathsf{Exp}(\mathcal{A}) = 1] - \tfrac{1}{2}\bigr|.`
:::
::::

In Lean, the mass $`\Pr_R[\mathsf{Exp}(\mathcal{A}) = 1]` is converted to a real number with
`toReal`. The mass is at most $`1`, so the conversion loses nothing. Output $`1` is
`true`. In place of "finite and nonempty", the definition asks for an instance of
`SampleableType` on the key space, which gives the uniform draw of a key. It also asks
for decidable equality of messages, which the experiment uses to compare transcripts.

::::definition "uake_fixed_bit_exp" (lean := "AKE.UAKE.FixedBitExp") (uses := "uake_exp")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair AKE.UAKE.FixedBitExp -docstrings
Let $`\Pi` be a UAKE scheme over a monad $`m` whose key space $`K` is finite and
nonempty, let $`\iota` be a monad morphism from probabilistic computations to $`m`, let
$`\mathcal{A}` be an adversary against $`\Pi`, and let $`c \in \{0, 1\}`. The _experiment
with fixed bit_ $`c`, written $`\mathsf{G}_c(\mathcal{A})`, is the computation in $`m`
given by the steps of $`\mathsf{Exp}(\mathcal{A})` with three changes:

1. step 1 draws no bit and puts $`b = c`;
2. step 4 outputs $`c` instead of $`1`;
3. in step 7, when no revealed session with $`T` has a transcript that matches the
   challenge transcript $`\tau^*` with first-speaker bit $`\beta`, the output is $`b'`
   instead of the result of the test $`b' = b`.

We call $`\mathsf{G}_0(\mathcal{A})` the _real game_ and $`\mathsf{G}_1(\mathcal{A})` the
_random game_.
:::
::::

In the real game the adversary receives the challenge key $`K_0`, and in the random game it
receives the uniform key $`K_1`. In Lean, the bit $`c` is a Boolean, with `true` for
$`1`, step 7 is `finalizeGuess`, and the key space carries an instance of
`SampleableType` in place of "finite and nonempty".

::::definition "uake_dist_advantage" (lean := "AKE.UAKE.distAdvantage") (uses := "uake_fixed_bit_exp, prob_comp_runtime, uake_advantage")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair AKE.UAKE.distAdvantage -docstrings
Let $`\Pi` be a UAKE scheme over a monad $`m` whose key space $`K` is finite and
nonempty, let $`R` be a runtime for $`m` with monad morphism $`\iota`, and let
$`\mathcal{A}` be an adversary against $`\Pi`. Let $`\mathsf{G}_0(\mathcal{A})` and
$`\mathsf{G}_1(\mathcal{A})` be the real and the random game, run with $`\iota`. The
_distinguishing advantage_ of $`\mathcal{A}` under $`R` is the real number
$$`\begin{aligned}\mathrm{DistAdv}_R(\mathcal{A}) := \bigl|&\Pr_R[\mathsf{G}_1(\mathcal{A}) = 1]\\ &- \Pr_R[\mathsf{G}_0(\mathcal{A}) = 1]\bigr|.\end{aligned}`
:::
::::

In Lean, the value is VCV-io's `Measure.boolDist` of the two measures, which is the
truncated difference $`|a - b|` from the definition of the bias, applied to the two masses
of $`\{1\}` and converted to a real number with `toReal`. Both masses are at most $`1`, so
the conversion loses nothing.

::::theorem "uake_advantage_eq_dist_advantage_div_two" (lean := "AKE.UAKE.advantage_eq_distAdvantage_div_two") (uses := "uake_advantage, uake_dist_advantage, prob_comp_runtime_canonical")
:::LibsignalVerifyBlueprint.pair AKE.UAKE.advantage_eq_distAdvantage_div_two (part := "signature") -docstrings
Let $`\Pi` be a UAKE scheme over $`\mathrm{ProbComp}` whose key space $`K` is finite and
nonempty, and let $`R` be the canonical runtime for probabilistic computations. For every
adversary $`\mathcal{A}` against $`\Pi`,
$$`\mathrm{Adv}_R(\mathcal{A}) = \tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathrm{DistAdv}_R(\mathcal{A}).`
:::
::::

In Lean, the key space carries an instance of `SampleableType`, and the theorem also
assumes decidable equality of messages, as the definitions do.

The proof uses the fact that the canonical runtime loses no mass. Under a runtime that
loses mass, the identity can fail: if every run of the three experiments fails, then
$`\mathrm{Adv}_R(\mathcal{A}) = \tfrac{1}{2}` and $`\mathrm{DistAdv}_R(\mathcal{A}) = 0`.

The bias and the two advantages differ by constant factors, and the factors hold only when
the experiment loses no mass. If $`\mu` is a probability measure on $`\{0, 1\}`, then
$`\mu\{1\} + \mu\{0\} = 1`, and so $`\mathrm{bias}(\mu) = 2\,|\mu\{1\} - \tfrac{1}{2}|`.
The bias of a game that outputs whether the adversary won is therefore twice its
advantage. Two open issues track the consequences.

* [Issue #29](https://github.com/Beneficial-AI-Foundation/libsignal-verify/issues/29):
  under a runtime that can lose mass, $`|\Pr_R[\mathsf{Exp}(\mathcal{A}) = 1] - \tfrac{1}{2}|`
  counts lost mass as a loss for the adversary. If half of the mass is lost and the
  adversary wins on the other half, then $`\Pr_R[\mathsf{Exp}(\mathcal{A}) = 1] = \tfrac{1}{2}`
  and the advantage is $`0`. An experiment that always fails has advantage $`\tfrac{1}{2}`.
  This does not affect the PQXDH theorems, which use the canonical runtime.
* [Issue #30](https://github.com/Beneficial-AI-Foundation/libsignal-verify/issues/30):
  the UAKE advantage of this repository is half of VCV-io's bias. A bound proved in one
  convention and used in the other is off by a factor of two.

The types differ as well. The bias takes values in $`[0, \infty]`. The advantages are real
numbers. Where a theorem combines the two, as the PQXDH theorems do with the IND-CCA
advantage of the KEM, it converts the bias to a real number with `toReal`.
